00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.300
How do they actually get
the, well, Weaviate set up?

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.710
BOB VAN LUIJT:
Yeah, so there are

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:13.980
two ways how you can do that.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.438
So one is if you want to try
it out or play around with it,

00:00:16.438 --> 00:00:18.140
we have Docker
containers available,

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.310
so you can run it even
on your local machine.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.160
But if you want to scale it to--

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.000
for big use cases, we have Helm
Charts and Kubernetes classes

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.530
ready that you can just--

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.602
with the push of a button,
you can run it out of the box.

00:00:30.602 --> 00:00:32.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So let's
talk about the first case,

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.100
where they just have
a Docker container.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:34.460
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yeah.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:35.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI: How
is it available to them,

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.352
and then what should
they do with it?

00:00:37.352 --> 00:00:39.018
BOB VAN LUIJT: One
of the things that we

00:00:39.018 --> 00:00:41.090
do is that we have
the machinery model.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.620
So that is pre-trained.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.320
That lives on Docker Hub.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.840
Also Weaviate itself
lives on Docker Hub.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.450
So you just literally
run a Docker Compose app,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.850
and you get everything, and
then you start running it.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.400
There's really nothing
more that you have to do.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.212
And a question that I get lots--

00:00:57.212 --> 00:00:59.670
people ask me, like, yeah, but
where do I need to train it?

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.050
And the answer is, you don't.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.944
We'll make sure that's
all happening for you.

00:01:03.944 --> 00:01:04.819
We train it on there.

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:06.080
We train it on Google Cloud.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.080
We deploy it to Docker Hub.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:09.770
That's it.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.890
It's just you click
Docker Compose app,

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.137
take a sip of coffee,
and you're good to go.

00:01:15.137 --> 00:01:17.470
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So all of
that made available to them?

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:17.670
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yes.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.462
MARK MIRCHANDANI: But
then the second model

00:01:19.462 --> 00:01:21.470
is you're kind of hosting
that for them, right?

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.970
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yes,
that is correct.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.800
So we also host it for them.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.420
Which, to be honest,
under the hood,

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.080
it's pretty much the same thing.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.913
So it really depends
on what people want.

00:01:29.913 --> 00:01:31.580
So they can use the
open source version,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.600
do everything themselves.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.310
They can use the hosted version,
where we host it for them.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:38.900
Or, we have a model
where we say we

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.480
help you set it up on,
for example, Google Cloud

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:42.660
and run it for you.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.160
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So when it
comes for you to actually build

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.970
that software, how
do you create it such

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.520
that it's available
for everybody?

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.870
BOB VAN LUIJT: Yeah,
so there are actually

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:51.860
three answers to that.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.970
So, first, we have,
of course, the model.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.300
So what we do is we
created a pipeline that

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.580
trains the model in
separate languages.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.570
So Weaviate comes
in eight languages.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.890
What we do, it starts on GitHub,
where the source code lives.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.570
And when we make
changes, or when

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.270
we want to create a new model--
because, of course, the data

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.103
that we're getting in
is also-- for example,

00:02:10.103 --> 00:02:12.770
Wikipedia is also
constantly growing.

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.530
We push that to Google Cloud.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.960
We train on CPUs.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.870
That has to do with the type
of model that we're using.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.040
In seconds, we spin up this
tremendously huge machine.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.650
I think we're talking
many dozens of CPUs.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:32.860
We're talking about, I
think, 800 gigs of memory.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.030
And it starts to
pull in all the data

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.840
sources, hundreds of
gigabytes of source material.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.840
It does the preprocessing,
it does the training.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.920
It compresses all of that into,
I think, in between 1 and 1/2

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.520
and 2 gigabytes of a model,
pushes it to Docker Hub,

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.183
shuts everything
down, and we're done.

00:02:52.183 --> 00:02:54.350
MARK MIRCHANDANI: How would
you say Google Cloud has

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:55.430
helped you build that?

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.170
BOB VAN LUIJT: So there
are two answers to that.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.560
So, first is more
the-- on a high level.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.130
Building this five
years ago, maybe--

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:05.570
I mean, especially
10 years ago--

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.030
maybe even five years ago
would've been impossible.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:10.910
I mean, it's just
impossible to do it.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.570
So these dozens of CPUs,
that amount of memory--

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.740
it would simply be impossible.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.770
So we are a small company.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.760
We're a startup, and
we can just build this.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.410
And not even just once--
we can do it every month.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.750
So the fact that these
tools are available to us

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:32.360
just at the click of a button
or a command in the command

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.550
line, that is fantastic.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.770
More practically,
that is the way

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:39.690
how Google Cloud has
organized the infrastructure.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.240
So we use the APIs
and G Cloud a lot.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:42.740
So.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.210
In batch scripts,
we use G Cloud.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:47.870
And in other situations,
we use the API ecosystem.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:50.810
It's just-- it's very
straightforward and very easy

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:51.360
to use.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.090
It's very easy to debug, to
understand what's going well,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.950
what's going wrong, releases
of underlying technologies

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:01.400
like operating systems-- that
just comes all out of the box.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:05.265
So it's just-- if I just tried
to think how we would have--

00:04:05.265 --> 00:04:07.640
should have done this five or
10 years ago, that's just--

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.940
that's impossible, I think.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.023
So that's how it helps us.

00:04:11.023 --> 00:04:12.732
MARK MIRCHANDANI: We
